"sjdevnull@yahoo.com" <sjdevnull@yahoo.com> wrote:

> A threaded news reader doesn't solve the problem,
> nor does Google.  It's entirely possible for your
> reply to arrive at my news server before the
> message you're replying to.

So? The cure for that problem is not to force the
unwilling or unable to quote context, the cure for
that problem is called "patience", patience on the
part of the recipient.

Once again, why does the prior poster have any
obligation to change his/her posting behavior to
cater to the problems caused by the recipients' own
choices?

> It's entirely possible that the message you're
> replying to has been expired already and is not
> available on my news server.

Google doesn't "expire" messages except at the
request of the original author. If the original
author chooses to expire messages, presumably that
is because the author does not want those messages
read after a certain time. Quoting parts of those
messages in subsequent articles is a clear violation
of this intent by the original author.

Once again, why should the prior poster be forced to
change his/her posting habits to ones violating an
implied copyright restriction of the original
authors, just to cater to your news reading habits?

> It's entirely possible that the message you're
> replying to has X-No-Archive set and is not
> archived on Google.

Umm, the above, but in spades. If messages are
marked "X-no-archive" the intent that the message
not survive to the future is even more clear, and
quoting that message as "context" in another message
is even more clearly a violation of that intent.

> And it's entirely possible that Google's broken
> attempt at threading doesn't actually capture the
> proper parent post, making the context very
> difficult to find.

Poor, poor pitiful you. I take the time to learn how
google works, and "very difficult" is a joke.
Finding a prior message in the archive is trivial.

Besides which, Google has gotten much better at
threading messages since it stopped using "subject
line/date-time-stamp" as a replacement for
"references:" threading, though this is still not an
argument about the merits of Google Groups, but
merely one using Google Groups as an example of why
"contenxt==none" now suffices to match the
"sufficient context" of the O.B.E. Usenet
etiquette guidelines.
 
> In short, the _design_ of Usenet is that you
> cannot be certain that any other post is visible
> at someone's server at the same time yours is.

Not really. That was the design in 1988 or so.
Today, I post an article on Google Groups, somewhere
in the US, and find it ready to read on MailGate, in
Italy, faster than I can switch windows and refresh
the article list to check for its arrival.

The ring time of Usenet in 1988 was around two weeks
out and two weeks back, as postings were stored by
one night's dial up phone transmittal, and forwarded
by one the next night. Today, that has been reduced,
for all but the poorest of participants, (who surely
cannot be spending their gruel money on reading
rgrn), to fractions of a second.

> Quoting context is not catering to some buggy
> newsreader or some luddite antiquated notion, it's
> simply having a basic awareness of the fundamental
> (intentional) design of Usenet.

Umm, nice if you insist that everything true at one
point of time be frozen in place and forever hold
true despite the advance of technology. Not so nice
if you are in charge of a news spool, and would
rather that context quoting be minimized to avoid
spool bloat, while the number of News groups has
grown from a few dozen Usenet ones to around a
hundred thousand ones hung from various hierarchies.
Not so nice if you don't want to be stuck using
habits which have been overtaken by events and are
no longer necessary (except to those who choose of
their own volition to cripple themselves with
free-but-braindead News reading technology) or
sensible in the light of modern technology.

  [I've had, up until a few days ago, extremely poor
  internet access; now I have a cable modem at home.
  I'm catching up on a year of several favorite
  online comic strips: Two Lumps, Schlock Mercenary,
  User Friendly, The Devil's Panties, etc. Does the
  author of each strip owe me context from the
  previous strip, day after day, year after year?
  No. I'm expected to be able to carry sufficient
  context _in my head_ to read down the thread of
  strips, whether one after another, as now, or at
  one to two day intervals, normally. The human
  mind more than suffices for this task.]

Those who complain here in rgrn about lack of
context are thereby tagging themselves as dunces who
just cannot remember from day to day how a
discussion was proceeding. Does anyone owe them "the
gift of context" to overcome their mental
deficiencies? I doubt it. Most of life and personal
behavior is tuned for the normal case, and the
normal case is that a one line comment either falls
into remembered context, or else isn't important
enough to the read for the reader to care or
comment, and certainly not to comment in public and
thereby clutter up a newgroup with unflagged
off-topic material..

> It also makes your post readable if someone
> searches the archives and finds it, but the parent
> post was not archived.

Which, again, is only a choice of the prior post's
original author, presumably with the explicit desire
that those words do _not_ survive into history,
something violated by quoting them as context.

  My personal take on this is that use of
  X-no-archive is based on cowardice, from being
  burned by being held to one's prior statements
  pulled back and thrown in ones face from the
  archives, but I can't guarantee, in any particular
  reason, that such is the reason for forcing lack
  of archiving.

And as far as the need for quoted context goes, it
simply doesn't exist at all if the subsequent
posting answers only one point in the prior one. No
one should be so brain-dead as to be unable to read
the prior posting and discern what is being
answered.

Quoted context _is_ useful if multiple responses to
multiple sections of a prior posting are being
given, and so clarity as to which answer goes with
which prior material can be achieved by a short
snippet of context. That's how, and why, I use it
myself, both in Usenet and in email, but I have no
delusions that my habits should become legally
required to be imitated by others.

